        <strong>Late Chunking</strong> embeds the entire document first (up to 8,192 tokens) using long-context transformer models before applying mean-pooling across chunk spans, preserving global context without cross-chunk fragmentation. On the BEIR benchmark, Late Chunking achieves a <strong>+4.2% higher NDCG@10</strong> than Sentence-Window Retrieval while eliminating the 3x vector storage explosion associated with overlapping window buffers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 1: The Context Boundary Dilemma in Modern RAG -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">The Context Boundary Dilemma in Modern RAG Pipelines</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Traditional Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) splits raw documents into fixed-size chunks (e.g., 256 or 512 tokens) with an arbitrary overlap (such as 50 tokens) before vectorization. This "naive chunking" approach suffers from fatal semantic fragmentation: an embedding model vectorizing chunk <code>[i]</code> has zero visibility into the antecedents, pronoun references, or definitions established in chunk <code>[i-1]</code>. When a user asks a question whose answer depends on resolving an anaphora (e.g., "What was the operating margin of the company described in section 3?"), naive chunk embeddings fail to capture the target company name.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        To solve this boundary decay, the AI engineering community has diverged into two competing paradigms:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300 text-sm mb-6">
        <li><strong>Sentence-Window Retrieval (Metadata Context Inflation):</strong> Embeds individual sentences in complete isolation to maximize search specificity, but attaches a surrounding window of <code>k</code> sentences (typically <code>[-3, +3]</code>) inside the vector metadata to inject into the LLM synthesis context.</li>
        <li><strong>Late Chunking (Bi-directional Transformer Post-Pooling):</strong> Processes the complete document (up to 8k tokens) through an encoder model so that all token representations encode global cross-sentence attention. Chunk spans are segmented <em>after</em> the transformer's hidden layers, followed by span-specific mean pooling.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

