    <!-- Section 3: Architecture Deep Dive: How Late Chunking Works Under the Hood -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Architecture Deep Dive: How Late Chunking Operates</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        In standard sentence or semantic chunking, a text is segmented into strings S1, S2, ..., Sm. Each string is tokenized and processed independently through the transformer layers:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-4 font-mono text-xs text-slate-300 mb-4 overflow-x-auto">
        <pre is:raw>
# Standard Chunking Flow:
Doc -> Split -> [Chunk 1] -> Transformer -> Pooling -> Vector 1
             -> [Chunk 2] -> Transformer -> Pooling -> Vector 2
# Attention Matrix: Block-diagonal only. Token in Chunk 2 CANNOT attend to Chunk 1!
        </pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        In contrast, Late Chunking reverses the order of operations. The entire document is tokenized up to the maximum model context length (e.g., 8,192 tokens in <code>jina-embeddings-v3</code> or <code>nomic-embed-text-v1.5</code>). The full sequence passes through all transformer self-attention layers, producing contextualized token embeddings:
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-4 font-mono text-xs text-emerald-400 mb-4 overflow-x-auto">
        <pre is:raw>
# Late Chunking Flow:
Doc -> Full Document Tokenization (8192 tok) -> Full Transformer Layers
    -> Hidden States Matrix H in R^{N x d} (Full Cross-Token Attention)
    -> Slice H by span boundaries [start_1:end_1], [start_2:end_2]
    -> Mean Pooling over span slices -> Vector 1, Vector 2
        </pre>
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Because the hidden state of each token in chunk <code>2</code> has already computed cross-attention over all preceding and following tokens in chunk <code>1</code> and chunk <code>3</code>, the resulting pooled vector naturally encodes the macro-context of the document.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4: Python Implementation Blueprint -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Production Python Implementation: Late Chunking with PyTorch</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Below is a production-grade, zero-dependency PyTorch implementation using Hugging Face transformers:
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/90 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-cyan-300 overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <pre is:raw>
import torch
import torch.nn.functional as F
from transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModel

def late_chunking(model, tokenizer, document_text: str, chunk_spans: list[tuple[int, int]]):
    """
    Computes late chunked embeddings from full-document transformer hidden states.
    chunk_spans: List of (start_token_idx, end_token_idx) tuples.
    """
    inputs = tokenizer(document_text, return_tensors="pt", max_length=8192, truncation=True)
    device = next(model.parameters()).device
    inputs = {k: v.to(device) for k, v in inputs.items()}

    with torch.no_grad():
        outputs = model(**inputs)
        # Extract last hidden state: [1, seq_len, hidden_dim]
        last_hidden_state = outputs.last_hidden_state

    chunk_embeddings = []
    for start_idx, end_idx in chunk_spans:
        # Slice token span representations
        span_hidden = last_hidden_state[:, start_idx:end_idx, :]
        # Apply mean pooling across the specific span length
        span_pooled = torch.mean(span_hidden, dim=1)
        # Normalize to unit sphere for cosine similarity
        span_norm = F.normalize(span_pooled, p=2, dim=1)
        chunk_embeddings.append(span_norm.squeeze(0).cpu().numpy())

    return chunk_embeddings
        </pre>
