    <!-- Section 5: The Vector Database Bloat Calculus -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Vector Database Bloat: Metadata Costs vs Storage Scaling</h2>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        While Sentence-Window Retrieval scores respectably on BEIR, it creates severe economic friction when scaling to millions of chunks in production vector databases like Qdrant, Pinecone, or pgvector.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-base leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Consider a corpus of 100,000 technical manuals (averaging 50 sentences per document = 5,000,000 total sentences):
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300 text-sm mb-6">
        <li><strong>Sentence-Window Inefficiency:</strong> Each vector record must duplicate the text strings of the 3 preceding and 3 following sentences in its metadata payload. Storing 6 extra sentences per vector at 120 bytes each adds 720 bytes of redundant uncompressed JSON metadata per record. For 5 million records, that represents an additional <strong>3.6 Gigabytes of cold metadata storage</strong> and increased heap cache pressure in RAM during HNSW graph traversal.</li>
        <li><strong>Late Chunking Efficiency:</strong> Because the semantic vector already incorporates contextual field data, the metadata payload only needs to store the exact text of the chunk itself. No parent-child duplication or window buffer inflation is required.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 6: When to Choose Which Strategy -->
