        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white mb-2">What is the primary architectural difference between Late Chunking and Sentence-Window Retrieval?</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Late Chunking feeds an entire document (up to 8,192 tokens) through a transformer encoder first to capture global bi-directional attention before applying mean-pooling over target spans. Sentence-Window Retrieval chunks the text first, embeds individual sentences in isolation, and relies on database metadata to fetch neighboring sentences during prompt construction.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white mb-2">Does Late Chunking consume more vector database memory than Sentence-Window Retrieval?</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            No. Late Chunking generates exactly one dense vector per semantic chunk with zero redundant overlap. Sentence-Window Retrieval requires storing expansive window context metadata or duplicate parent-child nodes, inflating vector database storage by 216% to 300% for 100,000 documents.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white mb-2">When should production teams choose Sentence-Window over Late Chunking?</h3>
          <p class="text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Sentence-Window Retrieval is optimal when using standard embedding models with short context limits (512 tokens like text-embedding-3-small) or when retrieval queries target microscopic entities (like specific serial numbers or dates) that need localized context injection.
